let's talk to a former secretary of  state for international development rory  stewart and thank you  for being with us  well what do you make of the events of  the past 24 hours everything has just  moved with alarming  speed it's absolutely extraordinary  um  from the point of view of people in  kabul terrifying  kabul was of course the most modern  part of afghanistan it was a place where  women were most active with a very  educated population and as you will  remember yourself from visits to  afghanistan  uh almost the exact opposite of the kind  of ideology the taliban is bringing so  it was a place where many refugees had  fled to get away from the taliban  they've increasingly found themselves  trapped and now taliban fighters are  entering the city and it's a terrifying  terrifying time for afghans  and what are you doing that's an image  on your screen if there's an image on  your screen of kabul itself you can see  all the shops are closed  and you can see people who look and say  they're associated with the taliban  walking down the streets and any women  out now are in burkas that's not  something you'd normally see  in fact that's the very center of the  town that's looking right at the area  where i used to work and live  normally you would have seen women there  with uncovered heads everybody has put  on traditional shower comes people are  trying to adjust now immediately to the  taliban taking over the city and do you  think adjusting to the taliban means  accepting that women's rights will be  wiped out  yes i mean the taliban are talking a  moderate game at the moment but remember  fundamentally at its core the taliban is  an extremely  disturbing organization it's used  child's  suicide bombers it provided haven to  osama bin laden  many journalists have met taliban  commanders recently who have said quite  openly that they don't believe women  should go to school  and those images that you're showing  there on the screen are absolutely  chilling i feel like i've been taken  back 20 years to the couple that i saw  just at the end of the taliban period  and as you say um the last time 96 to  2001  we did see terrorist groups get a  foothold we did see the opium and the  heroin trade grow and refugees pouring  out could it could it be different this  time the taliban are claiming it will be  different  it's going to be i'm afraid very very  dangerous remember this is a very  fragile organization the taliban they  don't actually have that many people  they've taken power largely because the  u.s and britain suddenly took its air  support away from the afghan army  totally collapsed the morale of the  afghan army so they've taken much of the  country without fighting there aren't  very many of them their hold on power is  going to be quite fragile and the danger  there  is that they will take brutal reprisals  to try to keep that hold on power  so many afghans are worried about being  imprisoned about being killed some parts  of the country afghans are already being  executed  so yes we'll hope for the best but i'm  afraid this is terrifying  and parliament is to be recalled here  what are your thoughts about the  responsibilities of the british  government now in afghanistan what  should they be doing  well this has now become a development  in humanitarian crisis it's completely  heartbreaking but we now have to work  out how on earth we can support  tens of millions of afghans in desperate  condition  the government has entirely collapsed  there will be no healthcare taking place  doctors will have gone schools won't be  open  we will end up with trying to get food  through to people so the british  government's going to have to provide  hundreds of millions if not billions of  pounds worth of  development assistance with other  nations both for refugees and people  trapped inside afghanistan and we're  going to have to think about how we help  many many afghans get out of the country  i mean if you're an educated afghan  woman this is a terrifying moment  so to you know how far do you think the  british should go on that in helping  people who want to get out get out you  think we have a responsibility beyond  you know people who have had contact  with british forces and who have  supported british forces  absolutely because remember the british  forces were just a tiny part of our  presence in that country  there were as many foreign civilians as  there were soldiers  that's people working for charities for  ngos teachers etc many of these people  will of course be targets the taliban  millions of afghans worked alongside  foreigners in different endeavors to try  to rebuild the country together and took  huge risk doing so  huge risk in very simple ways i'm  thinking of an afghan that i was  thinking about this morning who set up a  very successful high school for girls  now he's not an interpreter for the  military forces but he's exactly the  kind of person who's going to get killed  i'm going to be killed because we  created this mess  essentially our very small true presence  was like the keystone arch holding the  whole building together  and when we removed it  the whole edifice has come collapsing  down we have to take responsibility for  that and just as america did  after vietnam where they took ultimately  two and a half million three million  refugees with other european nations out  of that country  we're going to have to think about  similar measures  and anthony blinken saying today that uh  you know they inherited a deadline and  couldn't change that do you do you  accept that  no no this is absolutely heartbreaking  stuff and i you know anthony blinken is  a very smart man but it's  it's it's ridiculous  they didn't need to do this and of  course they didn't intend any of this to  happen if you can see the statements  made by secretary blinken and by  president biden even in the last few  weeks  they were talking about it being orderly  and we've just got messages now i've got  friends trying to get to the airport u.s  civilians who've just got a message from  the u.s embassy saying that there's  firing happening now at the us airport  and that they are to stay safely in  place well what on earth that means when  as you showing on your screen  taliban pickup trucks are now driving  through the streets of kabul i mean this  is a catastrophe and no the u.s of  course could have remained because its  air power and its technology was  sufficient to prevent the taliban taking  a single town with very small presence  since 2016 when combat operations ended  they've effectively contained the  taliban as a rural presence and allowed  the afghan government to continue and  civilians get on with their lives  just finally rory you just to recap you  are saying are you that we have a moral  duty to resettle  afghans who want to come here where  where would you draw the line what are  you what is your thinking about  you know how we do that  well practically we have to make this an  international effort so i would expect  britain to lead  with other nations remember there were  at any time over 40 different nato  nations on the ground  so we should be working with france  germany scandinavia with the united  states with canada with australia and  others  as in fact happened after the vietnam  war  to work out how we can provide safe  passage and asylum for afghans that want  to leave but but be in no doubt we're  talking about many many millions of  people  and this is an entirely  horrifying and unnecessary tragedy but  it's a tragedy which we bear the  responsibility for for the reckless  actions the last few weeks  okay rory stewart uh appreciate your  time this evening thank you very much  thank you very much
 

